- (direction) Stage 2 pool mechanism (user design, k8s-example.md): Room=Cluster, Producer-pool-per-structure-kind=Node (e.g. spawns/extensions/mines as separate Nodes even though spawns+extensions are both today's 'fill' JobType), Worker Pool=Kubelet, worker=Pod, Control Plane=N/A (implicit in scheduler/config). Config sketch: per-Node { NumWorkers: fn()->num | num, Priority: fn()->enum | enum, Taints: string, balancer: LEAST_FULL | STICKY }.
- (decision) Vetted and adopted: resolves the earlier A-vs-B pool-mechanism fork as Option A (single global priority-ordered cascade), not a two-phase pre-allocator. Cascade becomes: Node Priority (now a live value, function-computable) -> within-Node balancer-selected target -> distance as final tiebreak. A Node's NumWorkers() gates eligibility live (colony-wide cap check), same structural slot AD-7's maxWorkers-per-Job check occupies today, just evaluated per-Node instead of per-Job-instance.
- (decision) Node granularity is finer than JobType: one Producer file still owns exactly one JobType (AD-1 unchanged), but each emitted Job now also carries a 'node' tag a Producer assigns per target (e.g. produceFill tags spawn-targets node:spawns, extension-targets node:extensions). Node is a new orthogonal per-Job field, not a new module or a new Producer-per-node.
- (decision) Taints/tolerations adopted (user asked to vet): a Node carries a small taint tag (e.g. FILLER, WORKER); each body-kind config carries tolerations (generalist tolerates everything; a specialist body's tolerations list only its own taint). Layers on top of existing body/requirements eligibility checks (AD-4), does not replace them -- routes/prefers, capability check still gates.
- (decision) balancer (LEAST_FULL, STICKY) generalizes today's ad hoc Harvester source-lock into a named, config-driven distribution mode and is consulted by BOTH Pulled-mode Matching (choosing among several open same-Node targets) AND Reserved-mode spawn-time target selection (control/spawn choosing which vacant mine slot to spawn a Harvester for) -- same concept, two call sites. assignmentMode (Reserved vs Pulled) stays a distinct, unchanged dimension: it governs WHEN/WHERE a Contract gets written (spawn-time vs match-time), orthogonal to balancer which governs WHICH specific target gets chosen.
- (decision) NumWorkers/Priority as functions: config.ts's successor table now contains executable pure functions, not only data. Rule: these functions take only an already-derived world-state-summary argument (population, NumContainers, NumExtensions, etc, sourced from the world/ snapshot) and read nothing else -- no Game/Memory calls, no closures over mutable state. This extends AD-10's Game-read discipline into config.ts, which was previously pure data with zero logic. Genuine new AD needed.
- (decision) Burstiness (FR-10) does not need a separate mechanism/AD: it collapses into NumWorkers() itself being a function that can spike under a condition (e.g. freshly-built empty extensions), same as any other environmental-pressure response. No separate 'burst allowance' structure.
- (decision) JobId grammar amends AD-4: type:targetId becomes type:node:targetId (e.g. fill:extensions:5f3a2b9). Rationale (user): pool/node occupancy count is then a live string-prefix count over current Contracts each Tick -- no separate node-lookup table, still fully AD-3-derived/stateless, resolves the earlier round-robin-state question as 'recompute from live counts' (not a rotating index/global cache).
- (question) StageGate (PRD FR-6/FR-7's discrete named-stage-ladder-with-gates concept) is likely superseded entirely by the continuous per-Node NumWorkers()/Priority() functions reading world-state directly (e.g. miner() returns NumContainers*MINER_CONSTANT -- naturally zero pre-container, nonzero at first container, satisfying FR-12 with no discrete stage concept at all). Open: does the colony still expose a discrete, decorative 'current stage/phase' label purely for console logging/observability (zero effect on scheduling), or is 'stage' dropped as a concept entirely, with only continuous per-Node functions governing behavior? Asked user.
- (decision) StageGate dropped (user: 'let's drop it', wants to revisit later, no integration path yet -- not a rejection, a deferral). Continuous per-Node NumWorkers()/Priority() functions are the only workforce-graduation mechanism in Stage 2. Logged to Deferred, not deleted from consideration.
- (question) User flagged: JobId embedding targetId + LEAST_FULL creates thrash risk -- a committed, traveling creep could get its Contract externally invalidated (target 'looks full' due to something OTHER than this creep's own delivery) and re-match to a different, also-volatile target, repeatedly ('walking circles'). Root-caused: FR-7 sticky binding already prevents a valid Contract from being re-matched every Tick, so the real vector is external interference invalidating a Contract mid-flight before the committed creep arrives -- specifically DYING-unload (Story 4.5), which delivers carried energy to 'the nearest needy structure' with NO awareness of existing fill Contracts, and can snipe a target another creep is already traveling to fill.
- (decision) General, balancer-agnostic fix (not a per-balancer patch): DYING-unload's target selection must prefer a needy structure with NO live incoming fill Contract over one that already has a committed filler -- i.e. respect existing Contract commitments same as normal matching does, instead of picking purely by distance/need. Combined with keeping per-target maxWorkers=1 for fill-type jobs (already Stage 1's spec-pinned value -- one target is never double-assigned by two regular fill creeps in the first place), this removes both thrash vectors without touching balancer logic at all. New AD needed: Contract commitments are authoritative over live structure state for any energy-delivery path, not just normal Matching.
- (version) screeps-launcher v1.17.0 (GitHub screepers/screeps-launcher releases, checked 2026-08-16) -- adds Node 24 support, compatible with existing Node 24 LTS pin. screeps private-server engine ~v4.3.0 (npm registry, checked 2026-08-16, published ~3 months prior). Bound into Stack table, AD-14.
- (event) Spine distilled inline (not subagent -- parent held full coaching context, solo/tight stakes). ARCHITECTURE-SPINE.md amended in place: AD-4/AD-5/AD-7 amended, AD-11/12/13/14 added, AD-1/2/3/6/8/9/10 unchanged. Capability map, Stack, Structural Seed, Deployment&environments, Consistency Conventions all updated. Ready for input reconciliation + reviewer gate.
- (event) lint_spine.py: 0 findings. Adversarial lens (inline): 5 findings, all fixed (Node-name typed union added to AD-11; undefined-node-lookup behavior pinned; Reserved-mode NumWorkers() authority pinned in AD-7; Priority evaluation frequency pinned once-per-Node-per-Tick; world-state-summary type ownership pinned to world/ in AD-12). Version lens: pass, both new Stack rows web-verified 2026-08-16. Reviews written to reviews/review-adversarial-s2.md, reviews/review-version-s2.md.
- (event) PRD reconciliation (inline): reconcile-prd-s2.md written. Full FR/NFR/Non-Goal coverage confirmed. Terminology drift found (not a gap): S2 PRD's FR-6/7 'stage gate' language, FR-9 'round-robin/greedy' language, and FR-11 'stage-scoped' language are stale against the resolved architecture (continuous Node functions, LEAST_FULL/STICKY balancer). Offered to sync PRD wording to user; not applied automatically since PRD is status:final.
- (event) Triage: no blocking open items -- PRD terminology sync is an offer, not a blocker for downstream (bmad-spec / bmad-create-epics-and-stories can consume the spine as-is). Renderings: none -- user confirmed spine-only deliverable at kickoff. External handoffs: none configured.
